From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-input: a reset keyboard has no LED lit (#784)

What: when a virtio keyboard stops (the guest resets it: a restart inside the
VM, QEMU still running), its LED state goes back to none lit, and the UIs
hear it (qemu_input_handler_set_leds_mask, the LED notifier).

Why: the guest's next driver starts with every LED off and reports only
changes from there. Kept, the old state (Caps Lock on before the restart)
stayed lit for the UI for good, so OmacVM's Cocoa UI took the restarted
guest's Caps Lock for on (omacvm-cocoa-caps-lock.patch) and the VM typed
the other case from the Mac's.

Only keyboards (the handler takes keys) and only when an LED was lit; mice,
tablets and the multitouch device are not touched.

Upstream: upstreamable (VNC, spice and D-Bus read the same stale mask).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/keys/test-caps-lock.sh (with the patched sources:
the reset is there); the restart row of work/run-1114/mini-1114 caps-repro.
Applies after qemu-cocoa-precise-scroll.patch.

--- a/hw/input/virtio-input-hid.c
+++ b/hw/input/virtio-input-hid.c
@@ -227,6 +227,15 @@
     if (vinput->active) {
         qemu_input_handler_activate(vhid->hs);
     } else {
+        /*
+         * OmacVM (#784): a reset device has no LED lit, and the guest's next
+         * driver reports only changes from there. Kept, the old LEDs (Caps
+         * Lock on before a restart inside the VM) stayed lit for the UI.
+         */
+        if ((vhid->handler->mask & INPUT_EVENT_MASK_KEY) && vhid->ledstate) {
+            vhid->ledstate = 0;
+            qemu_input_handler_set_leds_mask(vhid->hs, 0);
+        }
         qemu_input_handler_deactivate(vhid->hs);
     }
 }
